{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1498687-could-the-vitae-theoretically-lessen-some-complications-of-inbreeding-in-revenants","id":1498687,"title":"Could the vitae theoretically lessen some complications of inbreeding in revenants?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-12-03T10:21:15-08:00","id":1498687,"title":"Could the vitae theoretically lessen some complications of inbreeding in revenants?","content":"That feels like <i>such</i> an awkward question to ask, but I wonder every time revenants turn up and the setting's supposed to be fucked up anyway, so what the hell I guess.<br /> <br /> I remember a manga I read in my teens where vampires, as part of how fucked up they were, practiced incest to keep their bloodlines undiluted by humans, and the enhanced health and healing capabilities given to them by their vampirism prevented any major health impairments and side effects in their offspring. Thus me wondering whether the same could apply to a fetus that is, to some degree, naturally vampiric and has some access to the healing and protean properties of the Blood. I don't think they'd be fully immune to complications, but perhaps less prone to them or at least prone to less severe complications.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-03T11:10:57-08:00","id":1498690,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar26836_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"26836\" data-vbnamecard=\"26836\">Rhywbeth</a> I\u2019m inclined to agree with you. I remember some of the write ups for the revenant families, especially the Bratovitch and the Zantosa, specifically implied that at least sometimes they practiced incest. I mean, maybe it was the designers\u2019 way of being edgy, but it also sort of makes sense, like you said.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-03T19:52:43-08:00","id":1498726,"title":"","content":"I would expect Revenant families, especially in the early days of a family when there's only a half-dozen or fewer revenants, to be fairly incestuous. I don't think that vitae would prevent health complications or birth defects, but it would make the people in question far more likely to survive and have kids. I would mostly assume that various types of flaws would be fairly common among Revenants, at least during the early days of their families.<br /> <br /> In the modern era, most of the Revenant families are large enough that I don't think there's going to be much incest or inbreeding going on so it would be more of an issue in the Dark Ages or earlier campaign settings than in the modern one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-03T21:36:52-08:00","id":1498737,"title":"","content":"I think complications of extensive inbreeding would occur and be another factor (besides family culture and being enslaved by vampires) in revenants' weirdness. But they would be not so much lessened by vitae as compensated for by the ways it enhances ghouls, such as fast healing, enhanced strength and resilience, and other Discipline-related powers. Plus, ones whose birth defects are too physically impairing probably don't survive to adulthood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-06T21:09:00-08:00","id":1499031,"title":"","content":"The canonical populations of each Revenant family seems to be around 250. I am no expert on population genetics, but depending on who you talk to there seems to be a consensus that a minimum of 50 people are necessary to prevent inbreeding and 500 to prevent genetic drift. Some scientists speculate you would only need around 160 people on a colony spaceship to keep people genetically healthy. So even a single Revenant family alone is probably OK. Probably lots of arranged marriages (family politics are probably brutal among the Revenants). That improves if you allow the occasional marriage with a different family.<br /> <br /> The World of Darkness mechanics were never intended to accurately simulate demographics issues. What happens if one member of a Revenant family marries another? What in-house Disciplines do they get? What about normal humans who are absorbed into the family? These are never explained despite the game stating that both happen. The decision to give each Revenant family their own unique Discipline set has more to do with the needs of an RPG (we want our different splats to have different powers, not just flavor text differences) than a realistic feature of the setting.<br /> <br /> I imagine the families mostly try to avoid inbreeding though it does happen on certain estates, and there is a certain amount of &quot;new blood&quot; that does come in from time to time.<br /> <br /> Vitae should not lessen the effects of inbreeding or other genetic diseases. But the benefits it provides in terms of Disciplines may compensate or outweigh any negative consequences in the short term at least. Too frequent inbreeding will likely eventually lead to a population collapse and extinction of that family. As an ST, I would probably include various signs of distinct genetic traits among Revenant families like the notorious Habsburg chin or the hemophilia of Queen Victoria's grandchildren to show there is a certain genetic degeneracy among them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}